t institutional subscriptions\n\n## Other ways to access\n\nLicence this eBook for your library\n\nInstitutional subscriptions\n\n## About this book\n\nThe book is aimed at people working in number theory or at least interested in this part of mathematics. It presents the development of the theory of algebraic numbers up to the year 1950 and contains a rather complete bibliography of that period.  The reader will get  information about results obtained before 1950. It is hoped that this may be helpful in preventing rediscoveries of old results, and might also inspire the reader to look at the work done  earlier, which may hide some  ideas which could be applied in contemporary research.\n\n## Table of contents (6 chapters)\n\n  1. ### Front Matter\n\nPages i-xi\n\nDownload chapter PDF\n\n  2. ### The Birth of Algebraic Number Theory\n\n     * Władysław Narkiewicz\n\nPages 1-61\n\n  3. ### The Turn of the Century\n\n     * Władysław Narkiewicz\n\nPages 63-93\n\n  4. ### First Years of the Century\n\n     * Władysław Narkiewicz\n\nPages 95-139\n\n  5. ### The Twenties\n\n     * Władysław Narkiewicz\n\nPages 141-187\n\n  6. ### The Thirties\n\n     * Władysław Narkiewicz\n\nPages 189-227\n\n  7. ### The Forties\n\n     * Władysław Narkiewicz\n\nPages 229-257\n\n  8. ### Back Matter\n\nPages 259-443\n\nDownload chapter PDF\n\n## Reviews\n\n“The book is a new masterpiece in the series of Wladislaw Narkiewicz’s books that are favourites of all researchers in number theory. … The book is truly recommended to all number theorists.” (István Gaál, zbMath 1416.11003, 2019)\n\n## Authors and Affiliations\n\n  * ### University of Wrocław, Wrocław, Poland\n\nWładysław Narkiewicz\n\n## About the author\n\nWladyslaw Narkiewicz is a Polish mathematician who is particularly active in the fields of (analytic) number theory, algebra and the history of mathematics. He received his PhD in 1961 and his habilitation in 1967 at the University of Wroclaw, where he also taught from 1974 to 2006 as a full professor. He is the author of several Springer books, among which the “Rational Number Theory in the 20th Century” and “The Development of Prime Number Theory”.  Narkiewicz held during his career various administrative functions at the University of Wroclaw, including Deputy Head of the Mathematical Institute, Dean of the Faculty of Mathematics and Physics and Vice Rector for Scientific Affairs. In 1968 he was awarded the Stefan Banach Prize.\n\n## Bibliographic Information\n\n  * Book Title: The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century\n\n  * Book Subtitle: From Hilbert to Tate\n\n  * Authors: Władysław Narkiewicz\n\n  * Series Title: Springer Monographs in Mathematics\n\n  * DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-03754-3\n\n  * Publisher: Springer Cham\n\n  * eBook Packages: Mathematics and Statistics, Mathematics and Statistics (R0)\n\n  * Copyright Information: Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2018\n\n  * Hardcover ISBN: 978-3-030-03753-6 Published: 29 January 2019\n\n  * eBook ISBN: 978-3-030-03754-3 Published: 18 January 2019\n\n  * Series ISSN: 1439-7382\n\n  * Series E-ISSN: 2196-9922\n\n  * Edition Number: 1\n\n  * Number of Pages: XI, 443\n\n  * Number of Illustrations: 1 b/w illustrations\n\n  * Topics: Number Theory, History of Mathematical Sciences\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * Algebraic Numbers\n  * History of Number Theory\n  * Algebraic Number Theory\n  * 11Rxx\n  * 01A60\n  * 11-03--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Forties: From Hilbert to Tate (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/330493189_The_Forties_From_Hilbert_to_Tate)\nciteturn656search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # The Forties: From Hilbert to Tate ...   * In book: The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century (pp.229-257) ... Władysław Narkiewicz\n\nChapter\n\n# The Forties: From Hilbert to Tate\n\n  * January 2018\n  * Springer Monographs in Mathematics\n\nDOI:10.1007/978-3-030-03754-3_6\n\n  * In book: The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century (pp.229-257)\n\nAuthors:\n\nWładysław Narkiewicz\n\nWładysław Narkiewicz\n\n\n## Abstract\n\n1. In 1947 R. Brauer [428] found a proof of Artin’s conjecture on the divisibility of Dedekind zeta-functions for Galois extensions, showing first that in Artin’s theorem about linear combinations of characters induced by cyclic subgroups the rational coefficients may be taken to be nonnegative. As a corollary he obtained for normal extensions L / K a representation of $(\\zeta _L(s)/\\zeta _K(s))^n$ as a product of Abelian L-functions. He pointed out that from the truth of Artin’s conjecture on the integrality of Artin L-functions this corollary would hold also for non-normal extensions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century | Mathematical Association of America (https://old.maa.org/node/2169894)\nciteturn656search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.7 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century ... Wladyslaw NarkiewiczPublisher: ... The book under review is a companion to the author’s earlier Rational Number Theory in the 20th Century: From PNT to FLT. ... Tate’s 1950 thesis is one of the highlights of Chapter 6 and serves as an fitting endpoint; it centers on an approach to Hecke’s \\(L\\)-functions that treats each prime separately via \\(p\\)-adic numbers.\n\n# The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century\n\nImage\n\n###### \n\nWladyslaw Narkiewicz\n\nPublisher: \n\nSpringer\n\nPublication Date: \n\n2019\n\nNumber of Pages: \n\n443\n\nFormat: \n\nHardcover\n\nSeries: \n\nSpringer Monographs in Mathematics\n\nPrice: \n\n119.99\n\nISBN: \n\n978-3-030-03753-6\n\nCategory: \n\nMonograph\n\n  * MAA Review\n  * Table of Contents\n\n[Reviewed by\n\nDavid P. Roberts\n\n, on\n\n02/24/2020\n\n]\n\nThe book under review is a companion to the author’s earlier Rational Number Theory in the 20th Century: From PNT to FLT. Both books are works of extraordinary scholarship: the earlier book had 6849 references while this one has 4482. In both books, each reference corresponds on average to about one-twentieth of a page of the main text. The pace is thus extremely brisk, with the general topic changing every page or two. In this way and others, the two volumes are reminiscent of Dickson’s classic three-volume History of the Theory of Numbers. However, the mathematics in Narkiewicz’s two books is much more modern, and thus much more sophisticated.\n\nWhile algebraic numbers in the first half of the 20th century certainly form the focus of the book under review, considerable attention is given also to both earlier and later times. A long Chapter 1 sets the stage with a survey of algebraic number theory through roughly 1890. Chapter 2 starts with Hilbert’s very influential 1897 Zahlbericht and gives equal attention to Hensel’s slightly later introduction of \\(p\\)-adic numbers. Chapters 3–6 proceed methodically from 1900 to 1950, with major themes including the development of class field theory and associated \\(L\\)-functions. Tate’s 1950 thesis is one of the highlights of Chapter 6 and serves as an fitting endpoint; it centers on an approach to Hecke’s \\(L\\)-functions that treats each prime separately via \\(p\\)-adic numbers.\n\nThe entire text alternates between a larger and smaller font. Text in the larger font focuses on the principal developments and stays within the current chapter’s time frame. Text in the smaller font centers on subsequent developments, often taken up to the present day. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century: From Hilbert to Tate (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/345038590_The_Story_of_Algebraic_Numbers_in_the_First_Half_of_the_20th_Century_From_Hilbert_to_Tate)\nciteturn656search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.8 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century: From Hilbert to Tate ... In this way, we answer completely Problem 6 and Problem 22 of Narkiewicz [34] for these families of number fields.\n\nBook\n\n# The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century: From Hilbert to Tate\n\n  * January 2018\n  * Springer Monographs in Mathematics\n\nDOI:10.1007/978-3-030-03754-3\n\n  * ISBN: 978-3-030-03753-6\n\nAuthors:\n\nWładysław Narkiewicz\n\nWładysław Narkiewicz\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe book is aimed at people working in number theory or at least interested in this part of mathematics. It presents the development of the theory of algebraic numbers up to the year 1950 and contains a rather complete bibliography of that period. The reader will get information about results obtained before 1950. It is hoped that this may be helpful in preventing rediscoveries of old results, and might also inspire the reader to look at the work done earlier, which may hide some ideas which could be applied in contemporary research.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\n## Citations (1)\n\n## References (0)\n\nOn common index divisors and monogenity of septic number fields defined by trinomials of type x^7+ax^2+b\n\nArticle\n\n  * Mar 2024\n\n  * Hamid Ben Yakkou\n\nWe study the index i(K) of any septic number field K generated by a root of an irreducible trinomial of type $F(x)=x^7+ax^2+b \\in \\mathbb{Z}[x]$. We show that the unique prime which can divide i(K) is 2. Moreover, we give necessary and sufficient conditions on a and b so that 2 is a common index divisor of K. Further, we show that i(K)=2 whenever 2 divides i(K). In this way, we answer completely Problem 6 and Problem 22 of Narkiewicz [34] for these families of number fields. As an application of our results, if 2 divides i(K), then the ring $\\mathcal{O}_K$ of integers of K has no power integral basis. We illustrate our results by giving some numerical examples.\n\nView--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe story of algebraic numbers in the first half of the 20th century: From hilbert to Tate (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/331113056_The_story_of_algebraic_numbers_in_the_first_half_of_the_20th_century_From_hilbert_to_Tate)\nciteturn656search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # The story of algebraic numbers in the first half of the 20th century: From hilbert to Tate ... As a consequence, we correct a faulty reference occurring in Narkiewicz' excellent book 'The story of algebraic numbers in the first half of the 20th century.\n\nChapter\n\n# The story of algebraic numbers in the first half of the 20th century: From hilbert to Tate\n\n  * January 2018\n  * Springer Monographs in Mathematics\n\nAuthors:\n\nW. Narkiewicz\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the author.\n\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (9)\n\n## References (1)\n\n... Hermite was apparently interested to extend this to the general case, i.e., that for every n ≥ 4 and D ̸ = 0 there are up to GL 2 (Z)-equivalence only finitely many polynomials f ∈ Z[X] of degree n and discriminant D. In Hermite (1857) he introduced a new equivalence relation (called by us 'Hermite equivalence', see Section 3) and proved in an ineffective way a finiteness result on the corresponding equivalence classes of integral polynomials of degree n and discriminant D. We show that GL($2,\\mathbb{Z}$)-equivalent polynomials are Hermite equivalent and that for polynomials of degree 2 or 3 the converse is also true. For $n\\geq 4$ we give an explicit upper bound, depending only on n for the number of GL($2,\\mathbb{Z}$)-equivalence classes of primitive, irreducible polynomials in $\\mathbb{Z}[X]$ of degree n going into a Hermite equivalence class. Further, for every $n\\geq 4$ we give in infinite paramatrized collection of pairs of polynomials (f,g) in $\\mathbb{Z}[X]$ of degree n such that f,g are Hermite equivalent but not GL($2,\\mathbb{Z}$)-equivalent. We point out that the results of Birch and Merriman, Gy\\H{o}ry, and Evertse and Gy\\H{o}ry are in general much more precise than Hermite's theorem. As a consequence, we correct a faulty reference occurring in Narkiewicz' excellent book 'The story of algebraic numbers in the first half of the 20th century. From Hilbert to Tate' (Springer, 2019), where GL($2,\\mathbb{Z}$)-equivalence and Hermite equivalence of polynomials were mixed up.\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Twenties: From Hilbert to Tate (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/330493270_The_Twenties_From_Hilbert_to_Tate)\nciteturn656search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # The Twenties: From Hilbert to Tate ...   * In book: The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century (pp.141-187) ... Władysław Narkiewicz ... The next section concentrates on the simplification of the class-field theory by Hasse and Chevalley, and the following sections concern i.a. the class-number and class-group of quadratic fields, the question of the existence of Euclidean algorithm in fields, the distribution of algebraic integers on the complex plane and infinite extensions of number fields.\n\n# The Twenties: From Hilbert to Tate\n\n  * January 2018\n  * Springer Monographs in Mathematics\n\nDOI:10.1007/978-3-030-03754-3_4\n\n  * In book: The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century (pp.141-187)\n\nAuthors:\n\nWładysław Narkiewicz\n\nWładysław Narkiewicz\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this chapter we present first the progress in the study of the structure of number fields, the central subject being the existence of normal and normal integral bases, and then consider some additive questions, mainly on sums of squares. The next section concentrates on the simplification of the class-field theory by Hasse and Chevalley, and the following sections concern i.a. the class-number and class-group of quadratic fields, the question of the existence of Euclidean algorithm in fields, the distribution of algebraic integers on the complex plane and infinite extensions of number fields.\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century | (https://www.ark.no/produkt/boker/fagboker/the-story-of-algebraic-numbers-in-the-first-half-of-the-20th-century-9783030037536)\nciteturn656search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.7 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th CenturyAv Wladyslaw Narkiewicz, 2019. ... From Hilbert to TateThe book is aimed at people working in number theory or at least interested in this part of mathematics.\n\n# The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century\n\nAv Wladyslaw Narkiewicz, 2019.Del av serien Springer Monographs in Mathematics.\n\n* * *\n\nFrom Hilbert to Tate\n\nThe book is aimed at people working in number theory or at least interested in this part of mathematics. It presents the development of the theory of algebraic numbers up to the year 1950 and contains a rather complete bibliography of that period. The reader